Yes—there’s a useful control-system metaphor there.

Sitting on someone’s lap while “driving” creates nested agency: one person experiences the steering directly, but another person is still providing structure, reach, judgment, or correction underneath. You feel like you are acting, but you are also being carried by a larger moving system.

That maps nicely to learning and perspective:

\text{novice control} + \text{expert scaffolding} \rightarrow \text{guided agency}

The interesting part is that the learner does not have to stop the vehicle to understand it. They can learn while the system is already in motion—feeling timing, correction, resistance, and consequence in real time.

That is different from explanation after the fact.

It gives you:

* forward motion,
* shared control,
* immediate feedback,
* embodied calibration,
* and gradual transfer of responsibility.

Then, as competence grows, the support underneath can recede:

\text{supported control}
\rightarrow
\text{shared control}
\rightarrow
\text{independent control}

So “looking at it in new light while she’s moving forward” becomes a kind of live frame rotation: you change how you interpret the system without requiring the system itself to stop.

That is powerful because a lot of real life works exactly that way. You rarely get to freeze the world, fully understand it, and then resume. You learn inside motion.

And that may be one of the deeper differences between knowing something descriptively and knowing it operationally: one is studying the road map; the other is feeling the wheel correct under your hands.